drumee-bootstrap (2.0.18) stable; urgency=medium

  * entrypoint/infra creates the directories drumee-infra SHIPS under /etc/drumee that the
    render cannot produce. infra.js writes rendered FILES; it does not recreate empty
    directories that are part of the package's file list — credential/crypto, where
    populate.js writes the RSA keypair, and credential/prosody. Natively they exist because
    dpkg unpacked them, which is exactly why this never showed up: `dpkg -S
    /etc/drumee/credential/crypto` names drumee-infra. In a render there is no unpack, so
    provisioning died on `ENOENT: open '/etc/drumee/credential/crypto/public.pem'` AFTER
    having created every database, the entity pool and all four system accounts.
    Taken from `dpkg -L drumee-infra` rather than a list written here, so a directory added
    to the package arrives without anyone having to remember it twice.

 -- Drumee Packaging <packaging@drumee.com>  Mon, 10 Aug 2026 16:40:41 +0200

drumee-bootstrap (2.0.17) stable; urgency=medium

  * entrypoint/media is now entrypoint/converter, and it does real work instead of
    refusing to start. Two modes:
    - "verify" self-tests the toolchain by actually converting, not by running
      --version on each binary: txt -> pdf (soffice) -> text (pdftotext) -> page count
      (pdfinfo) -> png (gm, via its ghostscript delegate) -> thumbnail -> zip (7z). A
      present binary is not a working one; soffice in particular produces no output and
      no error when its profile directory is unwritable.
    - "worker" still refuses to start until DRUMEE_CONVERTER_CMD names an entry point.
      How a job REACHES this container is a server-team question, and inventing a queue
      protocol in an entrypoint would bake a wrong contract into a published package.
  * The converter asserts its own isolation at startup: it reports the one credential it
    is allowed (redis.json, from credential_dir) and DIES if db.json or email.json is
    readable. distribution.md §2's claim is now enforced by the thing it describes.
  * entrypoint/infra writes a second, scoped copy of redis.json under
    credential/converter/. Docker's volume subpath mounts directories only, so there is
    no way to mount one file out of the shared credential directory — without a
    directory of its own, the converter would have to be handed db.json as well. Still
    one writer per credential: the same job, the same values, the same run.
  * HOME points at the scratch volume, because soffice picks its profile location from
    it and that is what lets this role take a read-only rootfs later.
  * entrypoint/infra exports DRUMEE_TLS_METHOD into create-local-certs, read back from
    debconf (this job just ran dpkg-reconfigure against the preseed, so debconf is the
    authority on what was answered). create-local-certs signs the apex and jit.<domain> from
    PRIVATE_DOMAIN, but the PUBLIC names — vendors.<domain> among them — only when that
    variable is exactly "self-signed", deliberately, so an ACME path never gets a
    self-signed file at a location where a failed issuance would then serve a certificate no
    client accepts. Natively postinst exports it before calling bin/install; this entrypoint
    calls create-local-certs ITSELF and the rendered drumee.sh does not carry the value. So
    no vendors certificate was written while infra.js had rendered 03-vendors.public.conf,
    which requires one, and nginx refused its entire configuration:
    [emerg] cannot load certificate ".../vendors.<domain>_ecc/fullchain.cer".
  * healthcheck/converter probes the TOOLCHAIN, not a listener — nothing connects to this
    role, so there is no port. It checks soffice, gm, gs and 7z are present and that the
    scratch directory is writable, which is the failure that produces no output and no
    error, and only then checks for a worker process (and only if one is named).

 -- Drumee Packaging <packaging@drumee.com>  Mon, 10 Aug 2026 14:23:36 +0200

drumee-bootstrap (2.0.16) stable; urgency=medium

  * entrypoint/dns places the rendered zone configuration into /etc/bind from a side
    mount, instead of the volume being mounted over that directory. Two measured
    reasons: bind9 ships /etc/bind/named.conf — the top-level file that includes the
    three rendered ones — and a mount replaces what is underneath it, so named died with
    "open: /etc/bind/named.conf: file not found"; and mounting the three files
    individually is not possible, because Docker's volume subpath mounts DIRECTORIES
    only and a single file fails with "not a directory".

 -- Drumee Packaging <packaging@drumee.com>  Mon, 10 Aug 2026 12:12:53 +0200

drumee-bootstrap (2.0.15) stable; urgency=medium

  * entrypoint/infra refuses to ship a rendered tree that embeds the render target as a
    path, and prints which files and which paths. Three instances of that mistake were
    found by grepping the tree, so the grep is now part of the job: it is the cheapest
    possible guard, and it fails where the mistake is made rather than in named or in the
    application hours later.
  * It also generates the TSIG update key. named.conf.local includes it
    unconditionally, and natively init-named creates it — a HOST step this job skips, so
    a container render produced a zone configuration pointing at a file nobody had
    created. A key is a file, not a host action, so producing it belongs here.
    drumee-role-infra's image gains bind9-utils for tsig-keygen.

 -- somanos.sar@drumee.com  Mon, 10 Aug 2026 12:02:17 +0200

drumee-bootstrap (2.0.14) stable; urgency=medium

  * entrypoint/schemas init now populates as well as bootstraps, because "initialise the
    database" means both halves — natively bin/install runs the bootstrap and then
    populate.js. A schema with no sys_conf, no entity pool and no system accounts is not
    a usable instance: the application answers 500 with
    `call undefined.get_fonts_faces()`, which is what an empty sys_conf looks like from
    the outside.
    .
    It runs setup-schemas' OWN populate, not a container fork. That became possible with
    drumee-schemas 2.7.4, which taught lib/utils.js and lib/schema.js to pass the
    connection flags from db.json instead of assuming a local socket — so one populate
    serves both channels, which is the point of docs/channel-parity.md.
    .
    Idempotent: an instance whose yp.sys_conf already has rows is left alone. Re-running
    would mint new system accounts and a second entity pool every time a run-once job
    restarted.

 -- packaging@drumee.com  Mon, 10 Aug 2026 11:18:03 +0200

drumee-bootstrap (2.0.13) stable; urgency=medium

  * entrypoint/app starts pm2 the way the native channel does, because pm2-runtime
    cannot be used here. As PID 1 its argv is inherited by pm2's cluster wrapper, so
    every main/service worker received the ecosystem path as its OWN argv and Drumee's
    argparse rejected it: `ProcessContainer.js: error: unrecognized arguments: ...`,
    sixteen unstable restarts, then "errored" — while the container reported running and
    the web role answered 502.
    .
    Four invocations were eliminated by measurement, not by reading docs: `start <file>`,
    the bare `<file>`, a .js wrapper around it, and --no-daemon (2.0.6). Fork-mode apps
    survive the extra argument; cluster-mode ones do not, which is why `main` looked
    healthy while the entire REST tier was dead.
    .
    So it runs the same `pm2 start <ecosystem>` that /usr/sbin/drumee runs natively and
    holds PID 1 with `pm2 logs --raw`. The daemon spawns workers with no CLI argv to
    inherit. It also fails fast if anything is errored after eight seconds, because a
    role whose REST tier is dead otherwise reports healthy.
    .
    The underlying fault is not pm2's: an application should not die because its
    supervisor added an argument. parse_known_args in the Drumee apps would let
    pm2-runtime work directly, and that is a server-team change.

 -- somanos.sar@drumee.com  Sat, 08 Aug 2026 18:12:44 +0200

drumee-bootstrap (2.0.12) stable; urgency=medium

  * entrypoint/app: pm2-runtime takes the ecosystem file and nothing else. With `start`
    in front of it, pm2 passes "start <file>" down to each application as its OWN argv
    — and the Drumee processes parse argv, so every one of them exited with
    `ProcessContainer.js: error: unrecognized arguments: start
    /etc/drumee/infrastructure/ecosystem.json` and pm2 gave up after 16 unstable
    restarts, while the container itself reported running. The bare form brings main,
    main/service and factory online; verified before changing the package.
    .
    That is the second correction to this one line (2.0.6 removed --no-daemon), and
    both were the same mistake: assuming a pm2 invocation instead of running it.

