drumee-bootstrap (2.0.11) stable; urgency=medium

  * entrypoint/schemas points FACTORY_DIR at the package. The init script defaults to
    /factory, which is where the deprecated image COPYs the schemas source; the package
    installs that tree under /var/lib/drumee/schemas/templates/factory. So init created
    the base databases and then died on "/factory/seed/yp.sql: No such file or
    directory" — having already made half the change, which is the worst place for a
    run-once job to stop.
    .
    Exported by the role rather than changed in the script, so the deprecated image
    keeps working unchanged, and checked before use so a missing tree is named instead
    of discovered five statements in.

 -- packaging@drumee.com  Sat, 08 Aug 2026 17:48:59 +0200

drumee-bootstrap (2.0.10) stable; urgency=medium

  * Parse the port out of db.json as digits, and say why a connection failed. A quoted
    JSON string ends at its closing quote so it extracts cleanly, but an unquoted
    number does not: "port": 3306 yielded "3306 " with the trailing space, and mariadb
    read that as a unit suffix — `Unknown suffix '' used for variable 'port'`.
    .
    Nobody saw that message, and that is the more important half. schemas/init's wait
    loop mutes its probe so 60 retries do not produce 60 errors, so the job waited two
    minutes and reported only "MariaDB not reachable" — with the actual reason
    discarded on every attempt. It now runs the probe once more unmuted before giving
    up, which turns a class of failure (bad port, wrong password, unresolvable name)
    from a timeout into a message.

 -- packaging@drumee.com  Sat, 08 Aug 2026 17:17:32 +0200

drumee-bootstrap (2.0.9) stable; urgency=medium

  * Ships the container channel's database bootstrap, and entrypoint/schemas runs it.
    schemas-init.sh and container-populate.js moved here from deploy/docker as
    /usr/lib/drumee/schemas/{init,populate.js}: they are role logic, so they belong in
    a package where they are versioned, pinned and rollback-able (§9.2), not copied
    into an image. They also survive the deletion of that tree.
    .
    One copy, three consumers: this package, the deprecated schemas/populate images
    (repointed to a `pkg` build context), and scripts/build-seed.sh. Reusing the same
    vetted script for all three is deliberate — a fork is how the seed and the running
    stack drift apart.
    .
    entrypoint/schemas takes `init` or `migrate`, because §6 needs migrate separately
    re-runnable. init deliberately does NOT run setup-schemas/bin/install: that
    restores a mariabackup PHYSICAL snapshot with --copy-back, which cannot work when
    the database is in another container. It creates the base databases from the
    schemas repo's own templates/factory/ tree instead, over TCP as root.
    .
    The application credentials are read from the rendered volume's db.json rather than
    from the environment, which is what keeps one writer per credential: db.json is
    authoritative and already records where the database is. The domain comes from the
    volume's drumee.sh. Only DB_ROOT_PASSWORD arrives from the deployment, because
    creating databases is the one thing that needs root.

 -- packaging@drumee.com  Sat, 08 Aug 2026 16:59:07 +0200

drumee-bootstrap (2.0.8) stable; urgency=medium

  * One writer per credential. drumee_write_credentials wrote db.json and redis.json
    from the environment in BOTH the app and the schemas role, over a rendered tree
    those roles could not even see: the compose topology mounted a separate writable
    volume at /etc/drumee/credential, and a mount replaces what is underneath it, so
    the email.json and postfix.json infra-init had rendered were invisible. Confirmed
    with a two-volume mount test rather than deduced.
    .
    It is replaced by drumee_require_credentials, which verifies and fails with the
    name of the missing file — a missing credential otherwise surfaces as an
    access-denied from MariaDB with no hint about which file was absent. The infra job
    writes redis.json (the render does not touch it, and the shipped one points at
    localhost), and infra.js renders db.json honouring DB_*.

